    {
      "slug": "semi-gods-ceiling",
      "title": "Semi-Gods Ceiling",
      "year": 1490,
      "imageUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a3/Pinturicchio%2C_soffitto_dei_semidei%2C_1490%2C_roma%2C_palazzo_dei_penitenzieri.jpg",
      "imageWidth": 1456,
      "imageHeight": 1835,
      "story": "The Demi-Gods Ceiling is a painted coffered ceiling by the Italian Renaissance master Pinturicchio, dating to c. 1490 and housed in the last of the suite of reception rooms in the Palazzo dei Penitenzieri in Rome, Italy. It comprises 63 octagonal coffers in gilded wood, decorated with allegoric and mythological figures on a faux-mosaic background, and painted on paper.",
      "facts": [
        "The figures of the ceiling were inspired by medieval bestiaries and libri monstrorum, which contained hybrid figures such as sphinxes, armed tritons, satyrs, dragons, sirens and centaurs.",
        "The theme has hidden philosophical and humanist meanings, perhaps suggested by the literati who formed the cardinal's court.",
        "They were inspired by the sea thiasus featured in Roman sarcophagi and which was also used by Andrea Mantegna, whom perhaps Pinturicchio met in the building of the Belvedere Palace in the Vatican."
      ],
      "wikipediaUrl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semi-Gods_Ceiling",
